            "text": "Several Windows applications can convert a single photo into a 3D model without requiring an NVIDIA GPU, supporting AMD GPUs or CPU-based processing.\n\nHere are some notable options:\n\n*   **Modly** is a desktop application for Windows that leverages local AI to generate 3D models from images. It explicitly supports AMD GPUs on Windows through ROCm, automatically detecting the card and steering extensions to ROCm PyTorch wheels without requiring a separate ROCm installation.\n*   **RealityScan** is a photogrammetry solution that allows users to create detailed 3D models from images. Version 2.2 of RealityScan offers full AMD GPU support for Radeon, RDNA 4, and Ryzen AI Max on Windows, enabling fast 3D reconstruction.\n*   **DreamVision** is a fullstack application with an Image-to-3D mode that has been optimized to run on AMD Pro W7900 GPUs with ROCm 6.1. It can be run locally on a Windows machine.\n*   **Fudge3d** is another Windows 10/11 application that can turn a single photo into a textured 3D model using your own GPU. It emphasizes local processing, meaning generation happens on your hardware without sending images to a hosted service, and states no CUDA setup is required, suggesting broader GPU compatibility beyond NVIDIA.\n*   **Image-to-3d-model** is an AI-powered, one-click conversion tool available on the Microsoft Store for Windows. It utilizes the TRELLIS model to transform 2D images into high-fidelity 3D assets. While specific GPU requirements aren't detailed, Microsoft Store applications generally aim for wider system compatibility.\n\nOther tools like Hyper3D Rodin also offer image-to-3D conversion, often highlighting local processing capabilities or general GPU utilization without an explicit NVIDIA-only restriction. Online generators such as \"Image to 3D AI\" can also convert images to 3D models in a browser, which offloads the processing to their servers, thus not requiring a powerful local GPU."
          }
        ],
